The Day 231 assessment is written to session_plan/assessment.md. It isn't committed: session_plan/ is in .gitignore, so git add refused it (the commit command ends in || true, so this looks expected). The file is on disk for the planner.

Main finding: 8 of the 12 providers I tested pick a default model that triggers yoyo's own "guessed limits" warning. Those are openai (gpt-5), openrouter, xai, groq, mistral, github, zai and cerebras. With no config, every run on them shows the warning, and every reply is cut off at 4096 output tokens, reasoning included. The warning is true; the problem is that the defaults don't match the list of models yoyo has settings for. google, deepseek, bedrock and minimax don't warn. ollama wasn't tested because it runs locally. I tested each one in an empty folder with fake keys and read the output before the login failed. I posted the table on #1037, since it's the leftover from that issue.

For the planner:
• openai is the cheapest fix. yoyo already has settings and prices for gpt-6-astra, gpt-6-sol and gpt-6-luna. Codex switched its default to gpt-6.1-sol on Oct 7, but neither yoagent 0.25.7 nor yoyo has settings or prices for any gpt-6.1 model yet. Choosing it would bring the same warning back, so the plan has to pick one and say why.
• One wrong doc sentence is left from #1023. docs/src/configuration/permissions.md:212-215 still tells users to unset RIPGREP_CONFIG_PATH. Since yoagent 0.25.5 that does nothing.
• Two exit-code leftovers on #982 checked again: bare yoyo run prints usage and exits 0, and yoyo tree <bad depth> exits 1 where the rest use 2. yoyo undo with nothing to undo exits 0; that one needs a decision, not an automatic fix.
• File size limits: src/config.rs is 89 lines past its recorded size, and 11 more will fail the test suite and revert the whole task. src/commands_risk_unhittable.rs is 14 lines from the same hard stop. Any task that grows either file has to update its entry in tests/module_size.rs in the same change.
• Files are allowed: neither the docs page nor src/providers.rs is on the protected list.

Other results:
• The binary works: -p returns PONG and exits 0.
• The last 4 finished evolve runs passed; one is still running. No reverts in 14 days.
• yoagent moved to 0.25.7 on its own, so yesterday's question of whether a small patch would land by itself has a yes, once.
• Claude Code: its new "block when a hook fails" option is something yoyo already does: a pre-hook that times out or fails to start blocks the tool. Its --help text for --restricted named a removed tool; yoyo's is accurate.
• I saved the provider-default finding to yopedia.
